2. Error Handling 

Exception Handling 

Handle exceptions gracefully, preventing crashes and ensuring a smooth user experience. 

Encapsulate the code that may throw exceptions within a try block and catch specific exceptions or handle generic errors in the catch block. 

All exceptions need to be pushed to the custom exception handler and logged to custom object (depends on Project) 

Usage: LWC and Apex 

 

Graceful Error Messages 

Craft error messages that are easy to understand and provide actionable insights into the nature of the error. Include relevant details such as the source of the error. 

Ensure consistency in error message formatting and language throughout the application for a seamless user experience. 

Logging 

Include essential information in log entries, such as timestamps, severity levels, the source of the error, and relevant contextual data. 

Use logging levels appropriately (debug, info, warning, error) to categorize log entries based on their significance 

 

  

3. Accessibility using Semantic HTML 

Accessibility is vital in software development, ensuring that people with disabilities can use digital products effectively. Making your application accessible expands your user base and meets ethical and legal requirements like the ADA in the US and WCAG globally.  

Semantic HTML elements, conveying meaning to assistive technologies, play a key role. They make it easier for users with disabilities to navigate and understand content.  

Here's how you can enhance HTML accessibility without direct focus on accessibility: 

Use Appropriate HTML Elements: 

Instead of using generic div tags and spans, use semantic HTML elements such as <nav>, <header>, <footer>, <main>, <article>, <section>, and <aside>. 

Semantic elements provide context to assistive technologies about the purpose and structure of the content, aiding users in navigation. 

Provide Descriptive Text: 

Use descriptive text for links and buttons, indicating their purpose or destination. 

For images, use the alt attribute to provide alternative text that describes the content of the image. This is vital for users who rely on screen readers to understand visual content. 

Structure Content with Headings: 

Organize your content using heading elements (<h1> to <h6>) in a hierarchical order. 

Headings help users navigate through the content easily and provide a clear structure to the document. 

Ensure Keyboard Accessibility: 

Make sure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible. Users should be able to navigate through your website using only the keyboard. 

Use appropriate focus styles to indicate which element is currently focused, improving usability for keyboard users. 

Provide Focus Indicators:  

Ensure that interactive elements receive focus states when navigating with a keyboard. 

 

  

4. Custom Label and Configuration Driven 

Using a configuration-driven approach in software development involves storing configurable values, such as text labels, error messages, or settings, outside of the codebase. This method enables easy modification of these values without changing the underlying code. It offers benefits such as flexibility, maintainability, and scalability. 

Avoid Hardcoding:  

Refrain from hardcoding configuration values directly into the code. Instead, reference these values from the configuration source, allowing for dynamic updates and customization. 

Centralized Configuration Storage:  

Store configuration settings in a centralized location, such as a custom metadata, database, or external service. This centralization ensures consistency and facilitates easier management of configuration values. 

Use Custom Labels (for Salesforce):  

In Salesforce development, leverage Custom Labels to store text values, such as labels, error messages, and help text. Custom Labels provide a convenient way to manage and translate text across different languages. 

Security Considerations:  

Ensure that sensitive configuration settings, such as API keys or credentials, are securely managed and protected. Employ encryption or secure storage mechanisms to safeguard sensitive information. 

 

  

